        "body": "For meeting cadence review, the first step is to write the open decision, the requesting role, and the queue owner before anyone touches the operational record. A clean-looking status is not evidence that the underlying work is correct. A short written rule is easier to test than an unwritten habit. Confirm that the request belongs in this queue before assigning an owner. The meeting cadence review case note should show what the operational record held at the time and who reviewed it.\n\nThe record should show the source, the decision, and the surviving value in one place. Note the observation window; a case without a period cannot be compared later. Name the person who can close the question and the date by which the answer is needed. Record the requester and the promised response time. For meeting cadence review, compare the meeting series list with the destination value rather than relying on a status label.\n\nA case that moves between owners should keep both owner periods visible. If the team cannot reproduce the result from the record, the control is not finished. Name the trigger that brought the case into the queue. A clean-looking status is not evidence that the underlying work is correct. A sample meeting cadence review review checks the meeting series list, the decision log, and the surviving value in the operational record."
